#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180680 :tutorial/content "Let $f(x,y)$ be a function of two variables and $C$ be a path in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm dx +  f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\ncan be evaluated by parameterizing $C$ using the parameter $t\\in [a,b]$ and then applying a change of variables, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+f(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(f(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+f(x,y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe hardest part is often finding a suitable parameterization for curve $C.$ We'll discuss a number of different cases in this lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "ffb7dba6-a1f1-50a3-a8a2-c1bf8b0b495d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4271 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
